{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>These are the sons of Seir the Horite</b>. \"The Horite\" was the name of a man who was the father of a people called by his name, like the Emorite and the Perizite, as it is stated (Deuteronomy 2:22), \"whom He destroyed the Horite from before them.\" And he is called Seir after the name of the land, which is called Seir, and this name belonged to the land from Esav, who is called \"a man of Seir,\" from the day he went there onward. And likewise the name Edom came from him. But the verse distinguishes between them, for these are the sons of Seir the Horite, who were the original inhabitants of the land, not the sons of Seir the Edomite who came there afterward. And I will further explain the genealogy of the Horite in the Mishneh Torah (Deuteronomy 2:10) with the help of the Name. And Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] wrote (on Genesis 36:20): Scripture mentioned this to distinguish the genealogies of Seir and of Esav, because Israel would be commanded concerning the children of Esav. And Rashi wrote (on Genesis 36:24): There was no need to write the families of the Horite except for Timna, to make known the greatness of Abraham."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 36, :notes {0 "The abbreviation רב״י is resolved as Rabbi Abraham, referring to Ibn Ezra, whom Ramban names in the parenthetical gloss that follows."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:40:36.250676016Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}